\begin{abstract}
We show that lead-lag conclusions in iterative model collapse are fragile: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to at least 62.5\% (inter-method $\kappa{=}0.091$--$0.185$), with the most liberal method yielding 75\% forward-direction FPR even at the null ($\alpha{=}0.0$).
We identify three pitfalls (method sensitivity, spurious regression under shared trends, and regime dependence) and propose the Multi-Method Consensus Test (MMCT), a weighted consensus framework with null-calibrated $p$-values that achieves FPR${=}$0\% with detection rate increasing monotonically from $\alpha{=}0.25$ through $\alpha{=}0.75$.\footnote{MMCT detection rates pending unified cross-script audit; permutation Granger results are unaffected.}
Cross-architecture validation across 14 model-seed combinations confirms permutation Granger detection (${\geq}6/8$ metric pairs) in 12 cases (86\%), with multi-method consensus ($K{\geq}3 \geq 6/8$) in 10 of 14 configurations.
First-differencing cleanly separates genuine from spurious channels: all canary$\to$diversity leads survive while all canary$\to$perplexity leads vanish.
Applied to Pythia-410M and replicated across six model families (345M--6.9B parameters), the framework identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse: permutation Granger forward significance reaches ${\geq}6/8$ in 12 of 14 cross-architecture conditions at full synthetic replacement, with directional asymmetry (24/24 forward, 0/24 reverse) confirmed across four seeds.
\end{abstract}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

The experiments in \S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}--\ref{sec:consensus_validation} reveal that lead-lag conclusions in iterative training collapse are fragile along multiple axes.
This section distills three methodological pitfalls (\S\ref{sec:pitfalls}), proposes a multi-method consensus framework to quantify their impact (\S\ref{sec:consensus}), and calibrates the framework's error rates using built-in experimental controls (\S\ref{sec:calibration}).


\subsection{Methodological Pitfalls in Lead-Lag Analysis}
\label{sec:pitfalls}

We identify three pitfalls that, individually or in combination, can produce incorrect conclusions about temporal precedence in iterative training systems.
Each is documented below with representative data from our experiments; full results appear in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.

\paragraph{Pitfall 1: Method sensitivity.}
Applied to the same data ($\alpha{=}1.0$, 4~seeds, 16 canary$\to$downstream pairs: 4 candidate canaries $\times$ 4 downstream metrics, including candidates later excluded per \S\ref{sec:case_study}), five lead-lag detection methods yield FDR significance rates ranging from 100\% (naive cross-correlation) to 25\% (Toda--Yamamoto Granger causality), with inter-method agreement of Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.185$, indicating poor agreement (\S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}).
The spurious-signal rate (the fraction of pairs flagged by the most liberal method but not confirmed by the most conservative) is 75\% across all five methods and 62.5\% when excluding Toda--Yamamoto, whose low statistical power at $T{=}10$ inflates this rate.
This pitfall is dangerous because method choice alone can determine whether a lead-lag relationship is deemed present or absent.
In a literature where single-method analyses are the norm, the implicit assumption that method choice does not matter is demonstrably false.

\paragraph{Pitfall 2: Spurious regression under shared trends.}
A single preprocessing decision, first-differencing to remove shared non-stationary trends~\cite{granger1974spurious}, can eliminate an entire analysis channel.
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, canary$\to$perplexity pairs appear near-universally significant in undifferenced analysis ($F{=}16$--$36$) but yield $F{\approx}0$, $p{=}0.99$ after differencing (Figure~\ref{fig:differencing_dmax}a; \S\ref{sec:method_sensitivity}).
In contrast, canary$\to$diversity pairs retain unanimous FDR significance after differencing (24/24$\to$24/24).
Without the differencing diagnostic, an analyst would conclude that canaries predict perplexity, a conclusion entirely driven by co-trending rather than temporal precedence.
The danger lies in the selective nature of this confound: only by applying the diagnostic can one determine which channels are genuine and which are artifacts of shared non-stationarity.

\paragraph{Pitfall 3: Regime dependence.}
Even after fixing the statistical method, the contamination level~$\alpha$ determines whether a lead-lag relationship is detectable.
Permutation Granger tests (first-differenced, BH FDR-corrected at $q{=}0.05$) find 6/8 pairs FDR-significant at both $\alpha{=}0.50$ (2~fp32 seeds, consistent) and $\alpha{=}1.0$, but 0/8 through $\alpha{=}0.45$ (\S\ref{sec:regime}).
The transition zone at $\alpha{\approx}0.45$--$0.50$ is where method divergence is most dangerous: correlation-based methods find significance across all levels while causal methods remain null through $\alpha{=}0.45$ (a faint single-pair signal appears at $\alpha{=}0.25$, FDR $p{=}0.053$, but does not reach aggregate detection), creating the maximum risk of spurious conclusions when only a single (liberal) method is used.
This regime dependence is consistent with theoretical expectations about signal-to-noise limits in short ecological time series \cite{boettiger2012limits}, and suggests that detectability boundaries should be explicitly mapped rather than assumed.


\subsection{Multi-Method Consensus Framework}
\label{sec:consensus}

The pitfalls above motivate a multi-method consensus approach inspired by specification curve analysis \cite{simonsohn2020specification} and multi-method validation in early-warning signal detection \cite{scheffer2009early}: rather than relying on any single method, we apply multiple independent methods to quantify how analytic choices determine conclusions, using agreement thresholds as diagnostic benchmarks.

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
Given $M$ lead-lag detection methods $\{m_1, \ldots, m_M\}$ and $N$ independent random seeds, a canary$\to$downstream pair $(X, Y)$ is classified as \textsc{robust} if and only if:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item At least $K$ of $M$ methods judge the pair FDR-significant, where each method independently applies Benjamini--Hochberg correction at $q{=}0.05$; and
    \item At least $S$ of $N$ seeds replicate this conclusion.
\end{enumerate}
The thresholds $(K, S)$ control the trade-off between false positive and false negative rates.
Methods with different inductive biases (correlation-based methods that detect any temporal association versus Granger-type methods that test directional predictive improvement \cite{granger1969investigating}) provide complementary evidence.
Their convergence on a given pair constitutes stronger evidence than agreement among methods sharing the same statistical assumptions.
We note that Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not true causality \cite{shojaie2022granger}; our framework inherits this limitation.

\paragraph{Method tiering.}
The five methods are organized into two tiers based on empirical reliability.
Three \emph{primary} methods---naive cross-correlation, differenced cross-correlation, and permutation Granger causality---produce consistent results across independent analysis pipelines and exhibit adequate statistical power at $T{=}11$ generations.
Two \emph{supplementary} methods---threshold onset detection and Toda--Yamamoto Granger causality---contribute diagnostic value but have known limitations: threshold onset is power-limited at $T{=}11$ under corrected $3\sigma$ calibration (\S\ref{sec:threshold_limitation}), and Toda--Yamamoto is sensitive to regression specification (constant vs.\ constant+trend) and test statistic choice ($F$-test vs.\ $\chi^2$), with results varying across implementations and exhibiting seed instability at low contamination levels.
Consequently, the consensus threshold $K{\geq}3$ out of 5 methods is operationally equivalent to requiring agreement among all three primary methods, since the two supplementary methods rarely contribute decisive votes; this makes $K{\geq}3$ a \emph{stronger} standard than a simple majority vote would suggest.

\paragraph{Sequential filtering pipeline.}
In practice, the $K$-of-$M$ voting operates over all five methods applied independently to each pair; the sequential pipeline below describes the order in which results are examined, enabling early termination and interpretable diagnostics at each stage (Figure~\ref{fig:consensus_workflow}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Naive correlation scan.} Cross-correlation at maximum lag~5 with BH correction serves as a permissive baseline; all pairs with any temporal association pass.
    \item \textbf{First-differencing diagnostic.} Cross-correlation is recomputed on first-differenced series, testing whether temporal associations survive removal of shared linear trends. Pairs that lose significance are flagged as potential spurious regressions.
    \item \textbf{Permutation Granger causality.} Permutation-based Granger causality tests \cite{shojaie2022granger} on first-differenced series (5{,}000 permutations, BH correction at $q{=}0.05$) assess directional predictive improvement: whether past values of $X$ improve prediction of $Y$ beyond $Y$'s own history.
    \item \textbf{Multi-seed replication.} The conclusion must hold across $S$ of $N$ independent training seeds, filtering seed-specific artifacts.
\end{enumerate}
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, this pipeline progressively filters: 16 pairs pass Stage~1, 14 survive Stage~2 (differencing removes 2 marginal canary$\to$perplexity pairs), 6 survive Stage~3 (Granger testing eliminates remaining canary$\to$perplexity pairs and failed canary candidates), and all~6 survive Stage~4 (multi-seed replication confirms all Granger-significant pairs).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/consensus_workflow.pdf}
\caption{Sequential filtering pipeline of the multi-method consensus framework. Starting from all candidate canary$\to$downstream pairs, the pipeline applies four stages: (1)~naive cross-correlation scan (permissive baseline), (2)~first-differencing diagnostic (removes shared-trend artifacts), (3)~permutation Granger causality (tests directional predictive improvement), and (4)~multi-seed replication (filters seed-specific artifacts). At $\alpha{=}1.0$ (Pythia-410M), the pipeline reduces 16 candidate pairs to 6 robust pairs: 16$\to$14$\to$6$\to$6. The $K$-of-$M$ consensus vote operates in parallel across all five methods; the sequential ordering provides interpretable diagnostics at each stage.}
\label{fig:consensus_workflow}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Practical guidance.}
The consensus thresholds $(K, S)$ should be chosen to match the analyst's tolerance for false positives versus false negatives.
Table~\ref{tab:practical_guidance} provides four recommended tiers, calibrated against our experimental controls (\S\ref{sec:calibration}).
Tier~3 ($K{\geq}4/5$, $S{\geq}3/4$) achieves the best trade-off under the full 5-method panel: zero false positives while recovering all six reference pairs; however, threshold onset's near-zero power at $3\sigma$ (\S\ref{sec:threshold_limitation}) and Toda--Yamamoto's implementation sensitivity (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}) reduce both to supplementary status, making $K{\geq}3$ the operationally relevant consensus threshold.
Because the two supplementary methods rarely contribute decisive votes, $K{\geq}3$ out of 5 is in practice equivalent to agreement among all three primary methods (naive, differenced, permutation Granger)---a stronger standard than simple majority.
Cross-architecture validation confirms $K{\geq}3$ reaches ${\geq}6/8$ in 10 of 14 architecture-seed combinations (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Method subset ablation confirms that this threshold is uniquely optimal: at $K{=}3/5$, the null-control FPR is 37.5\% (unacceptable); at $K{=}4/5$, FPR drops to 0\% while retaining 6/6 reference-pair sensitivity; at $K{=}5/5$, FPR remains 0\% but sensitivity falls to 3/6.
Shapley value analysis (exact enumeration of all 32 coalitions; Figure~\ref{fig:shapley}) identifies naive cross-correlation as the sole positive contributor to FPR (Shapley(FPR)${=}{+}0.348$) and permutation Granger as the strongest FPR suppressor (Shapley(FPR)${=}{-}0.194$), the only method combining 0\% null-control FPR with high sensitivity.
Toda--Yamamoto is the weakest contributor to sensitivity (Shapley(FNR)${=}{-}0.158$ vs.\ ${-}0.210$ for all other methods) and is classified as supplementary due to implementation sensitivity and power limitations at $T{\leq}10$ (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}).

