\paragraph{From ad-hoc consensus to formal inference.}
The uniform $K{=}4/5$ threshold is instructive but statistically insufficient.
Its FPR and FNR properties are descriptively appealing: at $K{=}3/5$, the two highest-FPR methods (naive at 75\% and threshold at 37.5\%) must recruit a third; since differenced cross-correlation (0\%) and permutation Granger (0\%) have zero forward-direction FPR, only Toda--Yamamoto (12.5\%) can contribute false positives, bounding $K{=}3$ FPR at ${\leq}$12.5\%; at $K{=}4/5$, any agreeing coalition must include at least two zero-FPR methods, achieving FPR${=}$0\%; at $K{=}5/5$, Toda--Yamamoto's low power at $T{=}10$ inflates FNR to 50\%.
Moreover, threshold onset's near-zero power (\S\ref{sec:threshold_limitation}) and Toda--Yamamoto's implementation sensitivity (\S\ref{sec:ty_limitation}) reduce both to supplementary status; nonetheless, $K{\geq}3$ consensus---operationally equivalent to agreement among all three primary methods---reaches ${\geq}6/8$ in 10 of 14 cross-architecture conditions (\S\ref{sec:generalization}), confirming that multi-method agreement remains robust despite the reduced effective panel.
However, null-calibrated permutation testing reveals that even this seemingly reasonable threshold yields $p{=}0.16$, failing to reach significance at $\alpha_{\text{test}}{=}0.05$.
The MMCT weighted consensus (\S\ref{sec:mmct}) resolves this gap: FPR-inverse weighting provides formal $p$-values grounded in the empirical null distribution, maintaining FPR${=}$0\% with detection rate increasing monotonically across contamination levels.\footnote{MMCT detection rates pending unified cross-script audit; permutation Granger results are unaffected.}
